=== EDUCATION PROGRAM (/education) ===
/education holds printable study material written on top of the lab. It is a SHELF, not a book:
one self-contained folder per subject (education/<track>/), shared tooling in education/tools/,
and chapter numbering that RESTARTS inside each track.

  education/README.md         the hub - track table. Keep this path; its GitHub URL was shared
                              externally with an employer.
  education/CONVENTIONS.md    HOW to write. Read before touching study material.
  education/METHOD.md         HOW to run a track - plan, build, break, investigate,
                              document. Read before starting or running one.
  education/tools/            shared build pipeline. Track is the FIRST ARGUMENT:
                              build_docx.py <track> [chapter numbers]   (--list to enumerate)
                              figcheck.py <track>
  education/<track>/          chapters + diagrams/ images/ docx/ [manifests/] [app/] scratch/
  education/docx/README.md    stub only - preserves an externally shared link. No builds here.

RULES:
1. READ education/CONVENTIONS.md BEFORE writing or editing any educational material, and follow it.
   It is the authority on chapter structure, diagrams, figure legibility, the Word build and the
   highlight pass. Do NOT invent a new format per track.
1b. READ education/METHOD.md BEFORE starting or running a track, and follow it. It is the
   authority on HOW the work is done: plan (declare the traps up front), build (scripted and
   re-runnable, verified from INSIDE the guest), break (drills chosen for what they mean at
   3am), investigate (the surprises ARE the deliverable), document. Reading CONVENTIONS alone
   tells you what a chapter should look like, not how to earn the material in it.
2. If a convention must change, change it in CONVENTIONS.md; if a working practice must change,
   change it in METHOD.md - NEVER fork either into a track README.
3. ONLY DOCUMENT WHAT ANDREW ACTUALLY RAN. This is what makes the material worth anything.
   A research-only chapter is allowed but MUST declare that in its opening paragraph (worked
   example: track 1, chapter 7). Where real output varies between runs, say so in the text.
4. Tracks are self-contained. A track never relative-links into another track; cross-references go
   through education/README.md. Tooling is shared and never copied into a track.
5. Anything in a track's scratch/ is GITIGNORED (education/*/scratch/). Never tell the reader to run
   something that lives there - a fresh clone will not have it.
6. Each study track gets its OWN phase file (phases/phaseX_<track>.md) and follows the MANDATORY
   PHASE PROCESS below. Track READMEs are reader-facing indexes; phase files hold the working record
   (decisions, blockers, what was actually run).
7. Weight material toward OPERATIONAL reasoning - failure modes, what you do at 3am, which instincts
   make an incident worse - and tie each concept to a consequence for the system being studied.
8. METHOD.md is a FLOOR, NOT A CEILING. Deviating for a specific topic is allowed and expected.
   But if the deviation WORKS, fold it back into METHOD.md IN THE SAME SESSION - an improvement
   that is not written down is lost, and the next track silently reverts to the old way.

