=== GIT REMOTES & COMMIT ROUTING (READ BEFORE ANY PUSH) ===

Same model as the Capricorn project (unified_ui_DEV_PROD_GCP): SAFE content to GitHub,
EVERYTHING to GitLab. NO git-crypt / NO encryption. Pushes are ALWAYS explicit.

This repo has TWO remotes:
- origin → GitHub  (PUBLIC):  git@github.com:fiberoptix/home-lab-setup.git
    The curated showcase. Secrets are .gitignore'd and NEVER reach GitHub.
- gitlab → GitLab  (PRIVATE): http://root:<GitLab root pw — see PASSWORDS.md>@gitlab.gothamtechnologies.com/production/home-lab-setup.git
    The full private mirror. Gets EVERYTHING in plaintext (incl. ignored secrets).
    Auth = HTTP "wallet" (root:<pw> baked into the remote URL in .git/config), same as Capricorn.
    NOTE: the real password lives ONLY in .git/config (never pushed) and PASSWORDS.md (gitignored).

⚠️ ALWAYS PUSH WITH THE SCRIPTS. NEVER run a raw `git push` at either remote.

   ./push_github.sh     → PUBLIC GitHub (origin/main), curated tree
   ./push_gitlab.sh     → PRIVATE GitLab (gitlab/main), FULL plaintext mirror incl. secrets

   Both accept --dry-run, which runs every check and pushes NOTHING. Use it freely.
   (push_gitlab.sh was formerly gl-backup.sh; the old name no longer exists.)

RULES (CRITICAL):
1. NEVER commit or push without asking Andrew first.
2. BEFORE pushing, ALWAYS ASK: "Push to GitHub, GitLab, or both?" Then push only where told:
   - GitHub only (PUBLIC, curated 'main'):   ./push_github.sh
   - GitLab only (PRIVATE, full mirror):     ./push_gitlab.sh "message"
   - Both:                                    run both explicitly, GitLab FIRST
     WHY GitLab first (was "GitHub first" until Aug 19, 2026; flipped at Andrew's instruction):
       GitLab is the PRIMARY repo and the only complete copy of the project, including the ignored
       secret files. Back it up BEFORE running the push that cannot be taken back. A bad private
       snapshot is superseded by the next one; a secret that reaches PUBLIC GitHub is disclosed the
       instant it lands, and push_github.sh's gates are a filter, not a guarantee. Do the recoverable
       thing first and the irreversible thing second.
       (The only argument for the old order was cosmetic: push_gitlab.sh stamps each snapshot with
        `main @ <sha>`, so GitHub-first made that sha publicly resolvable. Tidiness, not safety.)
   Preview either with --dry-run first. push_github.sh will ask for typed confirmation;
   it REFUSES to push unattended unless given --yes.
3. GitHub (public) must NEVER contain sensitive information. There is no encryption — safety
   comes from .gitignore. These stay ignored and therefore NEVER land on GitHub:
   PASSWORDS.md, github_credentials.md, proxmox/credentials, proxmox/nas_credentials,
   /working/, /ddns/, *.pem, *.key, *.crt, .env*  (verify with: git check-ignore <file>)
   push_github.sh enforces this and FAILS CLOSED. Its four gates: origin really is GitHub and
   we are on 'main'; no TRACKED file has a secret-looking name; every known sensitive path that
   exists is still gitignored; and the outgoing diff contains no private-key blocks, no URL with
   an embedded password (the GitLab wallet!), and no AWS keys. If it blocks, FIX THE CAUSE —
   do not work around it with a raw git push.
4. GitLab = COMPLETE plaintext backup. ./push_gitlab.sh snapshots the ENTIRE working tree
   (tracked + ignored, secrets included, minus .DS_Store) onto gitlab/main WITHOUT touching the
   working tree, the real index, or the GitHub-bound 'main'. It also handles nested git repos
   (e.g. working/openclaw-ansible) so their files are captured rather than empty pointers.
   Never `git push gitlab main` directly — that would send only the curated tree, defeating the
   entire purpose of the mirror. GitLab will therefore always look "diverged" from main; that is
   BY DESIGN (separate snapshot history, fully disjoint — no common ancestor), not a problem to fix.
   Every snapshot is AUTO-STAMPED with `main @ <sha>` (plus `+dirty` if the tree has uncommitted
   work), so a snapshot can always be correlated back to the real history. Still pass a message —
   it is prepended — but forgetting it no longer discards the reason for the snapshot.
5. NEVER push the ignored/secret files to origin/GitHub. push_github.sh checks this for you, but
   if verifying by hand: `git status --ignored` and
   `git ls-files | grep -iE 'password|cred|\.env|\.key'` (must be empty) before pushing origin.
6. Push auth: GitHub via ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 (already trusted); GitLab via the HTTP wallet baked
   into the remote URL in .git/config — same credentials Capricorn/capricorn-docs use (GitLab
   root pw, see PASSWORDS.md). No SSH key needed. NEVER write that password into a tracked file.

